    "bounding box" : [ 91.8, 635.936, 520.254, 694.472 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "2. Shape of the Eigenfunction vs,E: To precisely understand how λs,E varies in E, we must not only understand the operator Fs,E but also the associated eigenfunction vs,E. This diﬀers from prior work, such as [Bap14], which circumvented the analysis of vs,E by instead estimating the action of Fs,E on certain test vectors (which loses careful control on λs,E for E ≈ E0). We approximate the eigenfunction vs,E in Section 8, where we begin by deﬁning an explicit function u˜ by"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 91.799, 553.856, 520.345, 630.79 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "u˜(x) = (t2 lnK)−1 · {|x| ≤ Ct2} + |x|−(2−s) · {Ct2 ≤ |x|}. (1.10) Observe that, if s is very close to 1, the function u˜(x) behaves as (t2 ln K)−1 for x ≤ Ct2 and as x−1 for x ≥ Ct2. This is the general behavior we expect for the eigenfunction vs,E. However, the precise location and nature of the crossover around x = O(t2), at which vs,E(x) changes from behaving as x−1 ∼ t−2 to as (t2 lnK)−1, is not transparent to us. So, instead of comparing vs,E directly to u˜, we will compare Fs,Evs,E to Fs,Eu˜. Speciﬁcally, as Lemma 8.5, we show that"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 216.119, 537.416, 520.344, 548.071 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "c(FEu˜)(x) ≤ (Fs,Evs,E)(x) ≤ C(FEu˜)(x), (1.11)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 91.799, 509.549, 520.285, 531.391 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "where the constants c and C are uniform in K. While useful, this does not explain how vs,E varies with E and, indeed, we do not know how to do this."
